  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+4)(3-x)(1-x),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,\\,1)\u22c3($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+4)(3-x)(1-x)$ are $x=-4,\\,x=3,$ and $x=1,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/691c37edf48a5d7807f7c43d73b0ac014358632a1957e3016f953af6f47c8f36.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-4,1)\u22c3(3,\\infty ).$",
